// Lesson 7.6 — Level Studio I
// Build day. Paper to playable. The cave is walked from the entrance to the
// goal the student chose in 7.5 (a lever door, a collectible, or the Cave Exit
// object) - there is no boss door anywhere in this project.
// Spikes and coins ship as prefabs but with NO instance in the Platformer
// scene, and Spikes.prefab has no collider. MobileEnemy has no collider and no
// Rigidbody2D, so a placed enemy does not fall - it simply does not move until
// the student's Unit 4 state machine can drive it.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>7.6 — Level Studio I</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Paper day is over (<a href="7.5.html">7.5</a>); build day is here. Today the sketch becomes
    tiles, the beats become rooms, and by the end of class the cave is walkable from the
    entrance to the goal <em>you</em> chose — unpolished, ugly, and real. Expect to spend most
    of the hour placing things that do not ship in this scene, because almost none of them do.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Studio Brief</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The contract for today:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Every beat on your sketch exists as painted tiles, from the entrance to your goal, with no gaps in the floor.</li>
        <li>Every beat is <em>passable</em>. You do not need it to be fun yet — you need it to be reachable.</li>
        <li>Every ladder is <em>two</em> objects: the painted strip, and the trigger zone you placed over it. Neither one alone is a ladder.</li>
        <li>Every spike, coin, enemy and turret is placed by hand, exactly where the sketch says. None of them are in this scene until you put them there.</li>
        <li>The goal works. Whatever you chose in <a href="7.5.html">7.5</a> — a lever door, a collectible, or an exit — it must actually function before you leave today.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Placing a prefab is not the same as placing a working object.</strong> The prefabs
        in this project are not finished gameplay. <code>Spikes</code> ships as a tagged picture
        with no collider unless you gave it one back in <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>. The
        <code>MobileEnemy</code> prefab carries an Animator and whatever script you wrote in Unit
        4, and no collider and no rigidbody at all. A coin you drag in is a coin that does
        nothing until it has the components <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> gave it. Every one of
        these is a real job, and they are the jobs that eat this studio session. Budget for them.
    </div>

    <p><strong>Build order — and it matters:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Grey-box the floor of the whole cave first.</strong> One long walk from the
            entrance to the goal, flat, using plain rock tiles on <code>Collision</code>. Run it.
            The walk is the spine of the level; everything else hangs off it. Do not decorate,
            do not place a single hazard.</li>
        <li><strong>Add the vertical structure, beat by beat.</strong> Ledges, walls, drops and
            ladders. After each beat, walk the <em>entire</em> cave from the entrance. The
            full-length walk is your build ritual — it is the only test that catches the problems
            you create three beats behind where you are working.</li>
        <li><strong>Place the threats last.</strong> Spikes, enemies, turrets — in that order if
            your sketch allows, because a spike is a tile's worth of work and a turret is a
            wired-up object. Threats are the most expensive thing to move, so they go in when the
            geometry has stopped changing.</li>
        <li><strong>Build the goal, and prove it works.</strong> Wire the lever's UnityEvent,
            place the coins, or place the exit. Then walk the whole cave one final time and
            finish the level the way a player would.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the goal, one more time.</strong> Before you build
        it, answer these:
        <ul>
            <li>What exactly does the player do at the goal, and which object do they touch to do it?</li>
            <li>If it is the lever: <code>Lever.cs</code> calls <code>Interact()</code> and fires a <strong>UnityEvent</strong>. Which object is on the receiving end of that event, and what method does it call? The lever never names the door — so what does the wiring look like in the Inspector?</li>
            <li>If the door is what blocks the player, what does the door need so the player cannot walk through it before the lever is pulled — and what changes about it after?</li>
            <li>How does the player know the goal is a goal? Name the visual, and remember you have no UI and no Canvas — everything you say, you say with tiles, sprites and geometry.</li>
            <li>Is the goal reachable by a player who has not found the ladder, the lever, or the double jump? If not, is that a gate or a mistake? A gate has a visible alternative.</li>
            <li>What happens after the goal is reached? If the answer is "nothing", decide whether that is acceptable today.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the walk ritual.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>How long does the full walk take, measured with a stopwatch rather than guessed? Write the number on the sketch.</li>
            <li>Which beat did you edit most recently? Walk from the entrance to <em>there</em> and back before you move on.</li>
            <li>What is the single thing you will look at on every walk — the floor seam, the ladder's bottom, the goal's visibility? Pick one and look at it every time.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Why Geometry Before Threats?</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>You built the floor first and the threats last. What would it cost you if you placed the turrets first and then discovered the floor was one tile too low?</li>
            <li>Every object you placed today had to be built rather than simply dropped in. What does that say about the difference between a prefab and a working game object?</li>
            <li>You have no UI and no Canvas, so everything you tell the player is told with tiles, sprites and geometry. Name one thing your cave says without a single word of text, and how it says it.</li>
            <li>The walk ritual catches problems three beats behind where you are working. Why is a habit that costs you two minutes every beat cheaper than a habit that costs nothing until the playtest?</li>
            <li>Your ladder is two objects — a painted strip and a trigger zone — and either one alone is useless. Name one other thing in this project that is also two objects pretending to be one.</li>
            <li>If you had to cut one beat from your cave to finish on time, which beat would you cut, and what would the cave lose?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Every trade has a build order, and every trade has learned it the expensive way. Read each
        of these for what it does in isolation, then work out which line of your own studio brief
        it is arguing with.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — A road crew's paving order:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Base course, then kerbs, then the surface. Pave the surface
// first and every later machine digs up your work.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — A theatre's fit-up schedule:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The set is flown in before the lights are focused.
// Focus the lights onto nothing and you focus them twice.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — An aircraft assembly station:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The wing is joined before the systems are routed through it.
// Nothing heavy goes in after the wiring.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — A formwork carpenter, stripping a mould (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The form is scaffolding. It is removed once the pour has set,
// and the face is finished last.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 5 — A restaurant kitchen's prep list (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Mise en place: everything cut and placed before the pan is hot.
// The pan waits. The prep never does.</code></pre>

    <ul>
        <li>Example 1 says the surface goes on last because later machines would destroy it. Which objects in your cave are "later machines" that destroy an earlier layer of work?</li>
        <li>Example 2 says focusing lights onto a missing set means focusing twice. Which of your beats would you have to re-polish if you moved its geometry afterwards?</li>
        <li>Example 3 says nothing heavy goes in after the wiring. Your lever's UnityEvent is the wiring — should you wire it before or after the door's collider exists? Defend the order.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Bug Hunt: Studio Faults</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>You drag a <code>Spikes</code> prefab into the cave and walk straight through it. Which component is missing, and do you fix it on the instance in the scene or on the prefab asset? Say which one the rest of your cave needs, and why.</li>
            <li>You place a <code>MobileEnemy</code> and it stands perfectly still forever. Read the prefab's Inspector and list what it actually has. What does your Unit 4 state machine need that the prefab does not ship with?</li>
            <li>You paint a ledge onto <code>No Collision</code> and fall through it, every time. Which of the two tilemaps carries the collider, and what is the quickest way to tell which one you painted into?</li>
            <li>Your ladder works for two tiles and then drops you. The painted strip is six tiles tall and the trigger zone is two. Which of the two objects defines how far you can climb, and what is the rule you just learned?</li>
            <li>You press E at the lever and nothing happens. Is that a wiring fault or a collider fault? Describe the test that tells the two apart, using only what is already on screen.</li>
            <li>You feel a snag while walking a beat you edited an hour ago. Why is finding it now cheap, and why is finding it during tomorrow's playtest expensive?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Checkpoint</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>End-of-studio checks. Every one of them answers to the walk ritual, not to your opinion.</p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Close the Day</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Walk the full cave out loud. Can you name every beat as you enter it — "teeth… climb… rest…"? Which beat did you hesitate on, and what does that hesitation tell you?</li>
            <li>Which beat do you most want watched tomorrow, and what is the single question you want answered about it? That beat goes first with your partner in <a href="7.7.html">7.7</a>.</li>
            <li>Which objects in your cave did you have to build or repair today rather than simply place? That list is the honest size of this studio session — does it match what you expected this morning?</li>
            <li>What did you cut from the sketch today because you ran out of time, and where will that work go instead — tomorrow, or nowhere?</li>
            <li>Commit and push: <code>"7.6: cave greybox complete, threats placed, goal wired"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Walk your whole cave from entrance to goal without anything stopping you</li>
            <li>Name each beat as you pass through it</li>
            <li>Prove the goal works — the lever fires its event and the door opens, or the collectible can be reached, or the exit switches scenes</li>
            <li>Explain why threats go in last and geometry goes in first</li>
            <li>Name every object you had to build rather than place</li>
            <li>Have a written playtest plan for tomorrow</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/index.html">Unit 7: Level Building &amp; Ladders</a> — the studio checklist</li>
        <li><a href="7.5.html">7.5 — Design a Big Cave</a> — the theory behind today's build</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-05/index.html">Unit 5: Levers &amp; Doors</a> — the UnityEvent wiring for your goal</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — colliders, triggers and Animator parameters</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="7.7.html">7.7 — Level Studio II + Cross-Playtest</a>, where a stranger walks your cave.</p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="7.6" title="7.6 Level Studio I — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
